WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.548
The Azure CLI is a practical bridge
between Bash automation and Azure services.

2
00:00:07.628 --> 00:00:14.149
Teams use it to list resources, publish
artifacts, deploy applications,

3
00:00:14.229 --> 00:00:19.594
update configuration, create storage,
change networking,

4
00:00:19.674 --> 00:00:26.301
start compute resources, query logs, and
support operational workflows.

5
00:00:26.381 --> 00:00:29.536
Those activities may look routine,

6
00:00:29.616 --> 00:00:34.244
but they can act directly on the Azure
control plane.

7
00:00:34.324 --> 00:00:40.425
Every Azure CLI command runs with a
tenant, subscription, identity,

8
00:00:40.505 --> 00:00:47.237
role assignment, selected cloud
environment, and runtime configuration.

9
00:00:47.317 --> 00:00:54.154
If a script uses the wrong tenant, wrong
subscription, wrong resource group,

10
00:00:54.234 --> 00:00:59.073
wrong region, wrong identity, or wrong
command input,

11
00:00:59.153 --> 00:01:03.361
a routine helper can create production
impact.

12
00:01:03.441 --> 00:01:10.488
A command that is harmless in a
development resource group may be high-impact in

13
00:01:10.568 --> 00:01:11.725
production.

14
00:01:11.805 --> 00:01:19.168
Secure Azure CLI usage means treating each
script as a cloud automation component.

15
00:01:19.248 --> 00:01:25.034
The script should know which tenant and
subscription it expects,

16
00:01:25.114 --> 00:01:31.530
which resource group and region are in
scope, which identity it is using,

17
00:01:31.610 --> 00:01:39.078
what resources it is allowed to affect,
and what output later automation will trust.

18
00:01:39.158 --> 00:01:46.416
It should not rely on whatever account
state happens to be selected when it starts.

19
00:01:46.496 --> 00:01:53.649
The defensive goal is scoped,
least-privileged, observable Azure automation.

20
00:01:53.729 --> 00:02:00.040
That means validating targets, protecting
credentials and sessions,

21
00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:06.011
invoking commands predictably, handling
JSON output carefully,

22
00:02:06.091 --> 00:02:12.086
guarding high-impact changes, and leaving
useful audit evidence.

23
00:02:12.166 --> 00:02:18.793
Small Bash helpers can be valuable, but if
they can change Azure resources,

24
00:02:18.873 --> 00:02:22.660
they deserve deliberate security design.
